After f63c16cf50 (cron-7c04faf754) removing a running cron job logs one
`WARNING cron.scheduler: Job '<name>' stopped on request: CronRunCancelled: …`
and books the ledger `cancelled`, but the owner's admitted turn still raised a
plain RuntimeError from `session_cron.execute`, so `_execute_claimed` logged
`ERROR gateway.session_authority: Admitted turn <id> failed` with a full
traceback for the same deliberate stop (hub agent.log 2026-11-13 19:36:32 and
2026-11-12 23:12:44).

Five whys: the ERROR comes from `_execute_claimed`'s catch-all `.exception`;
it fires because `execute` turns every failed cron result into a bare
RuntimeError; that loses the scheduler's expected-stop classification, which
only lived in `run_job`'s own log call (`_log_expected_stop`); nothing carried
it across the owner boundary.

Fix: `execute` raises `CronTurnStopped` (a RuntimeError with
`expected_stop = True`) when run_job's error is `CronRunCancelled: …` or
`CronShutdownInterrupted: …` — exactly the `<type>: <message>` text
`_job_error_message` builds for the two exceptions `_log_expected_stop`
quiets. `_execute_claimed` logs such a turn at INFO ("Admitted turn <id>
stopped: …", no traceback). Settlement is unchanged: still
('The admitted turn failed.', 'failed') and the same pending_results.
Real failures (lost ownership, any other error) keep the ERROR + traceback.

Proof: new test_requested_cron_stop_is_not_an_admitted_turn_failure composes
`_execute_claimed` -> real `session_cron.execute` with run_job's except-branch
(message + `_log_expected_stop`). RED before the fix for CronRunCancelled and
CronShutdownInterrupted (ERROR at session_authority.py:1425, traceback through
session_cron.py:411 — the hub excerpt); the RuntimeError control keeps its
ERROR. GREEN after; every test file importing session_cron/_execute_claimed/
CronRunCancelled: 57 files, 444 passed. scripts/check --base origin/main ok.

Undo: revert this commit.
